= Human-computer interaction =

== Overview ==
'''Human-computer interaction''' (HCI) studies the design, use, evaluation, and social context of interactive computational systems for human use.

HCI combines computer science with psychology, design, ergonomics, accessibility, and social-science methods.

== Interaction ==
Interaction includes more than graphical interfaces.

People interact with computers through keyboards, touch, voice, gestures, sensors, haptics, automation, mixed reality, command languages, physical controls, and other modalities.

== Usability ==
Usability commonly concerns qualities such as effectiveness, efficiency, learnability, error recovery, and satisfaction for particular users and tasks.

Usability is important but is not the whole of HCI.

== User experience ==
Broader experience can include trust, emotion, aesthetics, engagement, values, accessibility, social context, power, privacy, and long-term effects.

A usable system can still produce a poor or harmful user experience.

== Human factors ==
Human perception, [[Attention]], [[Memory]], [[Cognitive load]], motor control, expertise, and expectations shape interface performance.

Design should fit human capabilities rather than merely expose machine structure.

== Evaluation ==
HCI uses methods such as:

* usability testing;
* controlled experiments;
* interviews;
* surveys;
* field observation;
* telemetry;
* participatory design;
* qualitative analysis.

Different questions require different methods.

== Accessibility ==
Accessible design aims to make systems usable across diverse abilities, devices, contexts, and assistive technologies.

Accessibility is not an optional visual polish layer.

